counterexample:
-- No counterexample exists under standard SQL as Q1 and Q2 are semantically identical.
-- The following inserts demonstrate the behavior, which would be identical for both queries.
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c3) VALUES (1, 'Apple');
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c3) VALUES (2, 'Banana');
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c3) VALUES (3, 'Cherry');

INSERT INTO table2 (c2, c1) VALUES (101, '1');
INSERT INTO table2 (c2, c1) VALUES (102, '3');
-- If table2.c1 could be NULL, it would affect both queries identically.
-- For example, if we could insert NULL (which is typically allowed for FK columns not explicitly NOT NULL):
-- INSERT INTO table2 (c2, c1) VALUES (103, NULL);
-- In this case, 'Banana' (c2=2) would not be in ('1', '3', NULL), so '2 NOT IN ('1', '3', NULL)' would be UNKNOWN (false).
-- Both Q1 and Q2 would return 'Apple' and 'Cherry' (if '2' was the only non-matching value and no NULLs were present in table2.c1).
-- With the current data, both queries would return 'Banana'.

