explanation:
{
  "explanation": "Both Q1 and Q2 aim to retrieve `c3` and `c4` from `table1` for those `c3` values that have all their corresponding `c6` dates in `table2` falling within the first quarter of 2019 (January 1st to March 31st). The core logic is in the subquery: `SELECT c3 FROM table2 GROUP BY c3 HAVING MIN(c6) >= '2019-01-01' AND MAX(c6) <= '2019-03-31'`. This subquery identifies `c3` values where all associated `c6` dates are within the specified range. Due to `GROUP BY c3`, this subquery will always return a set of distinct `c3` values. Q1 uses an `INNER JOIN` between `table1` and the result of this subquery, while Q2 uses a `WHERE c3 IN (...)` clause with the same subquery. In general, `INNER JOIN` with a subquery can produce duplicate rows if the subquery returns duplicate values for the join key, whereas `IN` implicitly handles distinct values. However, in this specific case, the subquery uses `GROUP BY c3`, which guarantees that it will return only distinct `c3` values. Furthermore, `c3` in `table1` is a `PRIMARY KEY`, meaning it is also unique. When both sides of an `INNER JOIN` on a single column are unique, and the subquery returns distinct values, the `INNER JOIN` behaves identically to an `IN` clause in terms of the final `SELECT` list (`c3`, `c4`). Therefore, under standard SQL and the given schema constraints, these two queries are logically equivalent and should always produce the same results.",
  "counterexample_dsp": "Given the schema constraints (e.g., `c3` is a `PRIMARY KEY` in `table1`, `c3` is a `FOREIGN KEY` in `table2`, and the subquery uses `GROUP BY c3`), the subquery will always produce a set of distinct `c3` values. Consequently, both queries are logically equivalent and will always produce the same results. A counterexample where Q1 and Q2 output different results is not possible under standard SQL. The provided test data will demonstrate this equivalence by including `c3` values that satisfy the date condition, some that do not, and some that have no corresponding entries in `table2`.",
  "counterexample": "INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (1, 'Apple', 10);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (2, 'Banana', 20);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (3, 'Cherry', 30);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (4, 'Date', 40);

INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 101, 1, '2019-01-15', 1, 1);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 102, 2, '2019-02-20', 2, 2);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 103, 3, '2019-03-10', 3, 3); -- c3=1: All dates within Q1 2019

INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (2, 201, 4, '2019-01-05', 4, 4);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (2, 202, 5, '2019-04-01', 5, 5); -- c3=2: MAX(c6) is outside Q1 2019

INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (3, 301, 6, '2018-12-31', 6, 6);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (3, 302, 7, '2019-02-15', 7, 7); -- c3=3: MIN(c6) is outside Q1 2019

-- c3=4 has no entries in table2"
}

